IMPORT `wra-definitions`

-- wra3-sch11.l4: the Welfare Reform Act 2012 (United Kingdom), 2012 c. 5, Schedule 11 (power to require consideration of
-- revision before appeal). Part wra3, layer 1. Written from inputs/ukpga-2012-5.txt, a plain-text rendering of
-- legislation.gov.uk (version 2026-07-01), not the official text.
--
-- Host Acts (not among the sources): VDPA = Vaccine Damage Payments Act 1979; CSA = Child Support Act 1991; SSRBA = Social
-- Security (Recovery of Benefits) Act 1997; CSPSSA = Child Support, Pensions and Social Security Act 2000; CMOPA = Child
-- Maintenance and Other Payments Act 2008. The paragraph numbers of Schedule 11 are not printed in the text. They are counted
-- by cross-heading and by the "is amended as follows" lines (VDPA paras 1 to 3; CSA paras 4 to 8; SSRBA paras 9 to 11; CSPSSA
-- paras 12 to 14; CMOPA paras 15 to 18): DERIVED and uncorroborated.
--
-- Each of six host provisions says: regulations may provide that, in prescribed cases, there is a right of appeal (or an
-- appeal may be made) only if the Secretary of State (or the relevant authority) has considered whether to revise the
-- decision. The shared reading (PLAN.md section 8, "the reading for seam 8"), the same one wra4 takes for section 102:
--   * the regulations are not among the sources, so "do the regulations require consideration first" is an input, and
--     NOTHING (unknown) or JUST FALSE (no such regulations) makes "is there a right of appeal" NOTHING (the right then comes
--     from the host Act's other provisions, which are not among the sources);
--   * with JUST TRUE and no consideration (or the further conditions the regulations specify unmet) it is JUST FALSE (a failed
--     "only if": a lawyer's reading (a), listed under Marginal readings in NOTES.md);
--   * with JUST TRUE and consideration done it is NOTHING (the "only if" is a necessary condition, and the Act does not say
--     what else gives the right).
-- "The regulations may in particular provide that that condition is met only where (a) the consideration was on an
-- application, (b) the issues were of a specified description, (c) any other specified condition is satisfied" is taken as one
-- input, whether the consideration satisfied the further conditions the regulations specify.
-- Schedule 11 paragraphs that make regulations procedural (VDPA 8, CSA 51A and 52, SSRBA 30, CSPSSA Sch 7 para 20(4), CMOPA
-- 49(2) and 53) and parenthetical insertions into host sentences whose lead-in is not printed (SSRBA 11(5)(c), CMOPA 50(4)(c))
-- are inert (reason 2).

§ `sch 11 the shared reading`

GIVEN `the regulations require consideration of revision before an appeal` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
      `the decision-maker has considered whether to revise the decision` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the consideration satisfied the further conditions the regulations specify` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`sch 11 there is a right of appeal, on the shared reading of the requirement of consideration first` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the regulations require consideration of revision before an appeal`
    -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text): the regulations are not among the sources
    WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING
    -- NOTHING-kind (silent): if the regulations do not so provide, the right of appeal comes from the host Act's other provisions, which are not among the sources
    WHEN JUST FALSE THEN NOTHING
    WHEN JUST TRUE THEN
        IF `the decision-maker has considered whether to revise the decision` AND `the consideration satisfied the further conditions the regulations specify`
        -- NOTHING-kind (silent): "only if" states a necessary condition; the Act does not say what else gives the right of appeal
        THEN NOTHING
        -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "there is a right of appeal ... only if [the decision-maker] has considered whether to revise the decision" and it has not (a failed "only if": a lawyer's reading (a))
        ELSE JUST FALSE

GIVEN `regulations provide that a purported appeal may be treated as an application for revision` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
      `there is no right of appeal against the decision in accordance with the regulations requiring consideration first` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`sch 11 a purported appeal may be treated as an application for revision, on the shared reading` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF `there is no right of appeal against the decision in accordance with the regulations requiring consideration first`
        THEN CONSIDER `regulations provide that a purported appeal may be treated as an application for revision`
             WHEN JUST TRUE  THEN JUST TRUE
             -- NOTHING-kind (silent): "may be treated" is a permission the regulations may give; if they do not, the text says nothing
             WHEN JUST FALSE THEN NOTHING
             -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text): whether the regulations so provide is not among the sources
             WHEN NOTHING    THEN NOTHING
        -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): the provision is for a case "where ... there is no right of appeal against a decision"
        OTHERWISE NOTHING

§ `sch 11 para 2 VDPA 4(1B)-(1C), 4(2)(c)`

-- src:3397 | (2) After subsection (1A) there is inserted—
